{
  "serverUrl": "http://SERVER_IP:3008",
  "apiKey": "REPLACE_WITH_AGENT_API_KEY",
  "wsPort": 3010,

  "_modes": "TWO CONFIG SHAPES — pick ONE. (1) LEGACY apiKey-mode (the three keys above): serverUrl + apiKey + wsPort; the agent dials SpanVault directly and presents Bearer <apiKey>, exactly as before. (2) JWT-mode: hubUrl + enrollToken and NO apiKey (see _jwtModeExample); the agent enrolls with the NetVault hub, presents its hub-issued JWT to SpanVault, and dials the hub-provided span ingest URL. hubUrl AND enrollToken are BOTH REQUIRED for JWT-mode. (Adding hubUrl+enrollToken alongside apiKey keeps apiKey on the data path and runs the hub channel as an additive side-channel — the Phase 2 opt-in.)",
  "_jwtModeExample": {
    "hubUrl": "http://SERVER_IP:3000",
    "enrollToken": "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLL_TOKEN"
  },

  "_wsTls": "OPTIONAL. When an app server has TLS configured on its agent WebSocket (SV_WS_TLS_CERT/KEY, DDI_WS_TLS_CERT/KEY) the hub advertises a wss:// ingest and the agent connects over TLS automatically — nothing is needed here for the traffic to be ENCRYPTED. wsFingerprints additionally VERIFIES the server: those certs are self-signed, so there is no chain to validate and the agent instead pins the certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint. Without a pin the channel is encrypted but unauthenticated (the agent logs UNPINNED and connects anyway, so upgrading the server first never strands an agent); with one, a substituted certificate is rejected before any frame is exchanged. Keys are per app because SpanVault and DDIVault present their own certificate when they run on different hosts — one shared pin would verify one and reject the other. On the usual single-host install one cert serves both listeners, so the same value goes in both. Colons and case are ignored, so paste openssl's output as-is. The installer writes this from -WsFingerprint / -WsFingerprintDdi.",
  "_wsTlsExample": {
    "wsFingerprints": {
      "span": "AB:CD:EF:...:12",
      "ddi": "AB:CD:EF:...:12"
    }
  }
}
